There is a tendency to tip more for better service, but it's not the biggest driver of differences in tipping. What are the bigger motivations and drivers of tipping? It's really social expectations and the desire to live up to those social expectations to avoid disapproval, gain liking, etc. Why do I say that? Again, in interviews with customers outside of restaurants, what I find is that the biggest predictor of tipping is bill size, which isn't surprising. There's this 15 to 20 percent tipping norm in this country. What may be surprising to people is to learn that that explains about 70 percent of the differences in tips left by different dining parties, which means that there's only 30% left to be explained by everything else.
人们确实倾向于为更好的服务支付更多小费，但这并不是导致小费差异的最大驱动因素。那么，给小费的更大动机和驱动力是什么呢？其实是社会期望，以及为了避免不被认可、获得好感等而达到这些社会期望的愿望。我为什么这么说呢？同样，在餐馆外的顾客采访中，我发现给小费的最大预测指标是账单金额，这并不令人惊讶。在这个国家，存在 15% 到 20% 的小费规范。人们可能感到惊讶的是，这解释了不同用餐群体所给小费差异的约 70%，这意味着只有 30% 的差异是由其他因素解释的。

