in the past, but you need to ask yourself who has that expectation and does it really matter? I get asked all the time, how much should I tip? And I don't want to tell people that because I'm not an authority. What basis do I have to tell you what you should do? And guess what? Emily Post, Amy Vanderbilt, etiquette experts don't really have that authority either. There is no god of tipping. Tipping norms don't come from on high. They evolve from the behavior of consumers and they become so common and so prevalent that we tend to judge people who don't follow that descriptive norm. And that's how we get the injunctive norm of tipping. What's important for people to realize is when people ask for tips, they want the tip and they expect you to give it to them.
但你需要问自己，谁有这种期望？这真的重要吗？我经常被问到：“我应该给多少小费？”我不想告诉人们，因为我不是权威。我有什么依据告诉你你应该做什么？你猜怎么着？Emily Post、Amy Vanderbilt 等礼仪专家其实也没有那种权威。没有所谓的“小费之神”。小费规范不是从天而降的。它们是从消费者的行为中演变出来的，变得如此普遍，以至于我们倾向于评判那些不遵循这种描述性规范的人。这就是我们如何得到小费的指令性规范。人们需要意识到的是，当人们要求小费时，他们想要小费，并且期望你给他们。

